# 07. Signal

A signal is an individual incoming observation from an approved source.

Conceptually:

```text
Signal {
    id
    source
    timestamp
    signalType
    value
    unit
    location
    quality
    provenance
}
```

---

# 08. Signal Quality

Every signal should have a quality assessment.

Possible states:

```text
VALID
DEGRADED
STALE
MISSING
INVALID
UNKNOWN
```

Signal quality must be visible to downstream intelligence.

---

# 09. Missing Data

AEGIS must distinguish:

```text
NO SIGNAL
```

from:

```text
NORMAL SIGNAL
```

This distinction is fundamental.

Missing information is uncertainty.

It is not evidence of safety.

---

# 10. Observation

An observation represents an interpreted or normalized signal.

Example conceptual structure:

```text
Observation {
    id
    sourceSignalIds
    observationType
    value
    timestamp
    location
    quality
    provenance
}
```

---

# 11. Anomaly

An anomaly represents a meaningful deviation from an established baseline or expected pattern.

Conceptually:

```text
Anomaly {
    id
    observationIds
    baselineReference
    deviation
    severity
    confidence
    detectedAt
}
```

---

# 12. Baselines

Detection requires context.

Possible baseline dimensions include:

```text
time
location
facility
population context
historical pattern
operational context
```

Baselines must be versioned.

A changing baseline must not silently rewrite historical interpretation.

---

# 13. Baseline Governance

The system must know:

```text
which baseline
was used
when it was generated
what data created it
which version produced the assessment
```

---

# 14. Detection Engine

The detection engine identifies signals that warrant attention.

It may use:

```text
threshold detection
trend detection
pattern detection
statistical anomaly detection
rule-based detection
approved machine-learning models
```

The architecture must support multiple detection methods.

---

# 15. Detection Output

Detection should produce:

```text
DetectionResult {
    anomaly
    method
    evidence
    confidence
    timestamp
}
```

Detection should not directly produce an operational intervention.

---

# 16. Detection Confidence

Confidence answers:

> How strongly does the available evidence support this detection?

It does not answer:

> Is this definitely a threat?

---

# 17. Event Correlation

Individual anomalies may be weak.

Multiple related observations may become meaningful when correlated.

Conceptually:

```text
Signal A
+
Signal B
+
Signal C
+
Temporal relationship
+
Spatial relationship
        ↓
Correlated Event
```

---

# 18. Correlation Engine

The correlation engine evaluates relationships between observations.

Potential dimensions:

```text
temporal
spatial
population
facility
operational
source
event history
```

---

# 19. Correlation Must Preserve Evidence

When an event is correlated, AEGIS must preserve the underlying observations.

Never create:

```text
"AI says outbreak"
```

without being able to answer:

```text
Why?
Which evidence?
Which sources?
Which time period?
Which model/rule?
What uncertainty?
```

